# Embeddable operator plane

Hatchery’s Controller is an **embeddable operator plane**: CI/CD, developer tools, and automation should incorporate it, not only sit beside a UI. Locked: [ADR-0021](../../docs/adr/0021-controller-embeddable-operator-plane.md). Planes: [architecture-nests.md](../../docs/architecture-nests.md). CLI: [ADR-0013](../../docs/adr/0013-hatchery-cli-launch-and-operator.md).

This is general product direction (external operators and tooling), not a single employer’s harness.

## Layers

```
External tooling / CI / agents
  → Controller operator surface (CLI now; HTTP operator API later)
  → Shared Nest orchestration (Nest id → factory)
  → Nest transport client  ≠  guest provision
  → Nest providers (libvirt / UTM / Hyper-V × Local | Remote)
```

UI, CLI, and any future HTTP operator API **share** orchestration. Do not invent a second path that talks to a hypervisor or Nest SSH directly from routes or one-off scripts.

## Surfaces

| Surface | Use |
|---|---|
| **Operator CLI** (`hatchery nest\|clutch\|vm\|hatch…`) | Primary embeddable surface today; Nest-scoped; in-process (no required HTTP server) |
| **UI / Flask `/api/...`** | Human Controller shell; **not** the frozen public contract |
| **HTTP operator API** | Future optional remote client surface; same lib as CLI when cut |

## Always ask

```
✅ Does this go through Nest id → factory / shared orchestration?
✅ Could CI or an external tool call this without the browser?
✅ Are product nouns stable (Controller, Nest, Clutch, Hatch, Cull, Snapshot, Revert)?
✅ Is Nest transport separate from guest provision?
✅ Will docs/CLI help stay enough to infer usage (no “read the Flask handler”)?

❌ Hard-wire libvirt / virsh / ad-hoc SSH in UI routes or CI helpers
❌ Treat today’s UI JSON routes as a versioned external API
❌ Employer-specific protocol as the core product surface
❌ HTTP-only operator that breaks local in-process CLI (ADR-0013)
❌ Second control path that bypasses Nest registry / transport
```

## When adding CLI, API, or Nest remoting

```
✅ Extend operator CLI / shared lib with clear Nest targeting and errors
✅ Prefer machine-friendly inspect/mutate output over time (stable exit codes; JSON when it helps automation)
✅ Document in docs/cli or architecture; ADR when locking a new contract
✅ Nest remoting (#209 / #215 / #345) hardens transport and jobs - same plane for UI and CLI

❌ One-off “SSH to Nest and run this” helpers for a single consumer
❌ Grow Flask UI routes as the de-facto automation API without a dedicated operator contract
```

Agents: pair with `cross-platform.mdc` (Controller vs Nest) and `hatchery-core.mdc` (naming).
